From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:28:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: reactions, sent-message ids and delivery/read ticks
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The webOS Messaging app shows reactions as badges on message rows, lets the
user react, and shows single/double ticks on outgoing messages. All three
need the plugin to talk to the transport (imlibpurpletransport) about
individual messages, which libpurple 2 has no API for, so this goes through
conversation data and custom signals the transport registers:

- Message ids. Each shown message - incoming, or a carbon of one sent from
  another client - gets the key "<chatId>:<messageId>" (Telegram message
  ids are only unique within a chat), stashed on the conversation as
  "webos-msg-id" right before serv_got_im/serv_got_chat_in or the carbon's
  write, where the transport picks it up as the row's serviceMessageId.
  Messages sent from this client are stored by the app's own send path, so
  for them the id is announced once TDLib has one: updateMessageSendSucceeded
  emits "webos-im-outbox-id" with the key and the plain text as a
  correlation hint.
- Incoming reactions. Telegram reports a summary per message, not
  per-sender events, so updateMessageInteractionInfo emits
  "webos-im-reaction-set" with the key and "<count> <emoji>\n" per unicode
  emoji reaction; the transport replaces the message's badges with that set,
  and an empty set clears them. Custom (sticker) emoji reactions are skipped.
  This is done next to the plugin's own updateMessageReactions(), which
  still writes its per-reaction notices into the conversation.
- Outgoing reactions. The transport emits "webos-im-send-reaction" for every
  account; the plugin connects to it once, filters to its own accounts, and
  calls addMessageReaction/removeMessageReaction (setMessageReactions is
  bot-only). Emoji variation selectors (U+FE0E/U+FE0F) are stripped first,
  or TDLib rejects "❤️" as an unknown reaction.
- Ticks. A send that succeeded means the message reached Telegram's servers:
  emit a "delivered" watermark for the chat. updateChatReadOutbox carries
  the last message the peer has read: emit a "read" watermark. Both go out
  as "webos-im-receipt-hwm" 2.5 s later, because the transport attaches the
  outbox id asynchronously and a watermark that arrives first matches no
  row; the watermark is cumulative, so the delay loses nothing.

Not for upstream: the signals and conversation-data keys are a private
contract with the webOS transport.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
